    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Pai Gow',
            subtitle: 'Ancient Tile Banking',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Pai Gow is an ancient Chinese gambling game played with a specialized set of thirty-two Chinese domino tiles. Each player is dealt four tiles which they must partition into two separate pairs: a high-ranking hand and a low-ranking hand. The objective is to outrank both of the banker's corresponding pairs, requiring deep knowledge of the complex and non-numeric tile ranking hierarchy.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Pai Gow (牌九) dates back to the Song Dynasty and is the ancestor of many modern partition games including Pai Gow Poker.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Up to eight players sit around a table; the role of banker rotates among players counter-clockwise.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'A unique set of thirty-two tiles where eleven specific tiles are duplicated to form matched pairs.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "32 Chinese dominoes, a 'Chung' marker for the banker, and three dice to determine the deal order.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Tiles are stacked in a 4x8 woodpile; dice are rolled to select which player receives the first stack.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Bonanza Pai Gow: Casino variant offering side bets on specific tile combinations and bonuses for high-ranking hands.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Commission Play: The house takes a 5% commission on all winning bets placed by players against the bank.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Player Banking: A rule allowing players to take turns acting as the banker, gaining the advantage of winning copy hands.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
